{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjardUBwPf5sh9PbfQjZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PBjohTZsTq3gEUdL4BKowL","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Start of line, before the first barline: a dotted quarter high on the staff (Eb4, 4th line), a quarter just above it (F4), then an 8th lower (Bb3). That is bar 22: (Eb4 quarter., F4 quarter), Bb3 8th. Next bar: two slurred 8ths low (Bb3–C4), then three more 8th-shaped signs. Two of these are the rests the edition has, drawn hastily like flags. That gives Bb3 C4 / rest / Bb3 / rest / Bb3, which is bar 23. Next bar, with pp below: 8th, rest, then a dotted quarter with a stroke (three repeated 8ths) on Bb3. That is bar 24: Bb3, rest, pp Bb3×4. Next: a dotted half on Bb3 with a stroke, meaning six repeated 8ths. That is bar 25. Next: 8th, rest, then a stroked dotted quarter. That is bar 26: Bb3, rest, Bb3×4. Next: a stroked dotted half on Bb3. That is bar 27: Bb3×6. Next: four 8ths on Bb3, a repeat barline, then two 8th rests. That is bar 28, with its repeat sign in the middle, as the edition notes. Last bar: rf, then a stroked dotted half low, below the staff (Eb3). That is bar 29: rf Eb3×6. The line ends there. Nothing contradicts the edition. The rests in bars 23 and 24 are hard to tell from flags, but the bar lengths and the repeat sign make them clear.","right":true,"insights":"The copyist shortens the repeated Bb3 8ths to stroked dotted halves and quarters. So the line looks nothing like the edition's run of 8ths, and the rests look like flags. The repeat sign inside bar 28 and the rf on the low note of bar 29 fix where the line ends. If the edition text listed repeated notes as abbreviated values (\"dotted half, 8th strokes\"), matching would be easier."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5423,"cach